.TH MITOSIS 1 "November 2026" "Mitosis" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mitosis \- content-addressed, suffix-chaining build system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mitosis
.RI [ -C
.RI TARGET-DIRECTORY ]
.RI [ -j N ]
.RI [ -Bvsqnkpd ]
.RI [ --trace ]
.RI [ --shuffle[=MODE] ]
.RI [ --eval-path ]
.RI [ -h|--help ]
.RI [ VAR = value ...]
.RI [ clean | target ...]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mitosis
is a build engine whose build graph is described in plain Tcl. Source and
intermediate files are related by suffix rules; each job is cached using its
command, tool identity, input contents, and recorded dependency contents.
The cache is content-addressed rather than based on modification times.
.PP
A project is described by
.BR build.mitosis.tcl ,
which is searched for in the current directory and then each parent directory.
Mitosis changes to the directory containing the project file before loading
it and building. The older name
.B build.celmag
is accepted when
.B build.mitosis.tcl
is absent.
.PP
Run
.B "mitosis -h"
or
.B "mitosis --help"
to display this manual through
.BR man (1).
The manual viewer uses the user's configured pager, normally
.BR less (1)
or
.BR more (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -C TARGET-DIRECTORY
Change to
.I TARGET-DIRECTORY
before searching for the project file.
.TP
.B -j N
Run up to
.I N
jobs concurrently. A value supplied as
.B -j4
is also accepted.
.TP
.B -B
Rebuild everything, ignoring cached results.
.TP
.B -v
Print full command lines.
.TP
.BR -s , --silent , --quiet
Do not print command echoes or the final summary. Output and diagnostics
from the executed commands are still preserved.
.TP
.BR -q , --question
Run nothing and print nothing. Exit with status 0 if the requested targets
are up to date, or 1 if a build would be needed.
.TP
.BR -n , --just-print , --dry-run , --recon
Print the commands that would run, but do not execute them. Jobs downstream
of a job that would run are also listed because whether an output would be
identical (and therefore cut off) is unknowable without executing it.
.TP
.BR -k , --keep-going
After a failure, continue running jobs that do not depend on the failed job.
.TP
.BR -p , --print-data-base
Print the rules, variables, planned files, and job graph, then continue with
the build.
.TP
.B -d
Enable debug output about planning and up-to-date decisions. This implies
.BR --trace .
.TP
.B --trace
Explain why each job runs, then print its command.
.TP
.B --shuffle[=MODE]
Change the order in which ready jobs are scheduled. MODE may be
.BR none ,
.BR random ,
.BR reverse ,
or an integer seed for a repeatable random order. The default when the
option is given without a value is
.BR random .
Shuffling helps expose missing dependency edges. Inputs to a fold/link job
are not reordered, because doing so would change its cache key.
.TP
.B --eval-path
Print the resolved evaluation-path directories and exit.
.TP
.BR -h , --help
Display the
.B mitosis(1)
manual using
.BR man (1).
Single-letter options may be clustered (for example,
.BR -nk ).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I VAR=value
Set a variable for this invocation. Command-line assignments override
values from the project file and finalization scripts.
.TP
.I VAR+=value
Append words to a variable after the project file and finalization scripts
have run.
.TP
.B clean
Remove build outputs, the build cache, and optional timing history.
.TP
.I TARGET ...
Build only the named targets and their dependencies. If no target is named,
all declared targets are built. If no targets are declared, Mitosis creates
one named after the project directory with
.B .out
appended.
.PP
Target source filters use Tcl
.B string match
patterns against originating source paths. Per-target
.BR -with ,
.BR -add ,
.BR -only ,
and
.B -not
options affect the final fold command only; compilation flags are shared.
.SH PROJECT FILE
The project file is plain Tcl and must select at least one digest with
.BR use .
The following commands are provided by the engine:
.TP
.B at DIR EXTS
Add source extensions found in
.IR DIR .
Multiple declarations are additive.
.TP
.B alias CANON NAMES
Treat the listed extension names as the canonical extension for edge matching.
.TP
.B relate FROM TO [CMD]
Declare a suffix edge. A
.I TO
value beginning with a dot appends that suffix to each input path. A bare
.I TO
folds all matching inputs into one output. Omitting
.I CMD
creates an ordering-only edge.
.TP
.B depfile EXT
Declare this output extension to contain make-style dependency information.
.TP
.B phase TYPE NAME
Assign a semantic phase name to jobs producing
.IR TYPE .
This is used by optional timing reports; the engine does not infer language-
specific phases.
.TP
.B use NAME
Load the named digest. There is no default digest.
.TP
.B set-default V X
Set variable
.I V
only if it has not already been set. An environment variable of the same
name is imported when the variable is first defaulted.
.TP
.B set-add V items...
Append items to list variable
.IR V .
.TP
.B target NAME [options]
Declare a final target. Target options are:
.RS
.TP
.B -only GLOBS
Keep only objects originating from matching source paths.
.TP
.B -not GLOBS
Drop objects originating from matching source paths.
.TP
.B -with {V X...}
Replace variables for this target's final fold command.
.TP
.B -add {V X...}
Append to variables for this target's final fold command.
.TP
.B -role ROLE
Select the fold-edge role filled by this target. The default is
.BR target .
.RE
.TP
.B default-target NAME
Declare the default target using the project's defaults.
.TP
.B finalize SCRIPT
Run
.I SCRIPT
after the project file and before planning. Finalization scripts are used
by evaluation-path layers to adjust the completed project configuration.
.PP
Command templates are split into words when declared. In a template,
.B $.EXT
expands to the input or output file of that type (a list for a fold),
.B $var
splices the named list variable into separate arguments, and
.B prefix$var
substitutes a variable within a word.
.SH CONFIGURATION AND PRECEDENCE
Environment variables are imported when a project or layer first calls
.BR set-default .
Configuration is loaded in this order:
.IP 1. 4
Evaluation-path layers, in name order.
.IP 2. 4
The project's
.BR build.mitosis.tcl .
.IP 3. 4
Finalization scripts registered by layers.
.IP 4. 4
Command-line assignments. These have final precedence; append assignments
are applied after finalization.
.PP
The first evaluation-path directory containing a layer name wins. An empty
layer file intentionally disables a same-named layer from later directories.
For digests, the first matching
.B digest/NAME.tcl
wins.
.SS Evaluation path
If
.B MITOSIS_EVAL_PATH
is set, its colon-separated directories are used. Otherwise Mitosis checks:
.IP \(bu 3
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mitosis
(or
.B ~/.config/mitosis
when unset),
.IP \(bu 3
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/mitosis/celmag
(or
.B ~/.local/share/mitosis/celmag
when unset),
.IP \(bu 3
.B /etc/celmag ,
and
.IP \(bu 3
.B $XDG_DATA_DIRS/mitosis/celmag
for each XDG data directory.
.PP
Only existing directories are used. Top-level
.B *.tcl
files are evaluation layers; files under
.B digest/
are named digests.
.SH TIMING DATA
Timing collection is disabled by default. Enable it by setting
.B MITOSIS_GRAPH=1
in the environment:
.PP
.nf
MITOSIS_GRAPH=1 mitosis
.fi
.PP
It can also be enabled in
.B build.mitosis.tcl
with:
.PP
.nf
set MITOSIS_GRAPH 1
.fi
.PP
When enabled, Mitosis appends records to
.BR .mitosis/timings.csv .
The CSV contains job, source-file, phase-group, and whole-run records.
Durations are in microseconds and timestamps are Unix milliseconds.
Group records distinguish wall-clock elapsed time from summed job work;
with parallel jobs, summed work may exceed the group's wall time.
Whole-run elapsed time includes planning and execution. Source line counts
refer to direct source files, not included headers. A digest labels its
phases with
.BR "phase TYPE NAME" ;
unlabeled jobs use the
.B generate
phase.
.PP
The CSV is append-only, does not participate in cache validity, and is
removed by
.BR clean .
Dry-run and question mode do not record timings. Consumers can read the CSV
without depending on Mitosis' internal cache format.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Build succeeded, or question mode found all requested targets up to date.
.TP
.B 1
Question mode found that a build would be needed.
.TP
.B 2
Build failure, command-line error, or other fatal error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B build.mitosis.tcl
Project build description, searched upward from the current directory.
.TP
.B build.celmag
Legacy project-file name, used only when
.B build.mitosis.tcl
is absent.
.TP
.B .mitosis/
Per-project cache and optional
.B timings.csv
history.
.TP
.B MANDIR/man1/mitosis.1
Installed section 1 manual. For a user install, the default
.B MANDIR
is
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/man
(or
.B ~/.local/share/man
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset).
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/mitosis/celmag
Default user evaluation data directory; see
.BR --eval-path .
.SH MANUAL INSTALLATION
The installer places this page at
.B MANDIR/man1/mitosis.1 .
For an ordinary user install, the default is
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/mitosis.1 ,
with
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
falling back to
.B ~/.local/share .
No root privileges are required. Override the manual hierarchy with
.B MANDIR
when installing to a nonstandard prefix:
.PP
.nf
MANDIR="$HOME/.local/share/man" ./install.sh
.fi
.PP
For a normal user install, the executable is placed in
.B ~/.local/bin .
With
.BR man-db ,
when
.B MANPATH
is unset, its default search path is derived in part from
.BR PATH :
for each executable path entry it checks the corresponding
.B ../share/man
hierarchy. Thus, when
.B ~/.local/bin
is in
.B PATH
and
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
has its default value, the page in
.B ~/.local/share/man/man1
is found by
.BR "man mitosis" .
.PP
If
.B MANPATH
is set, it overrides man-db's automatic search path. To add a custom
manual hierarchy while retaining the normal system paths, use a trailing
colon:
.PP
.nf
MANPATH="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/man${MANPATH:+:$MANPATH}:" man mitosis
.fi
.PP
The trailing empty component asks man-db to append its default paths; the
.B MANPATH
expansion also preserves any paths already configured by the user. Other manual-page implementations may use different
search-path rules; consult their
.BR man (1)
and
.BR manpath (1)
manuals. Check the active man-db path with
.BR "manpath" .
.PP
In a source checkout,
.B mitosis -h
uses the
.B mitosis.1
file beside
.BR mitosis.tcl .
Installed copies use the regular
.BR "man mitosis"
lookup, so the page does not need to be installed beside the executable.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the default target:
.PP
.nf
mitosis
.fi
.PP
Build with four concurrent jobs, forcing all outputs to be rebuilt:
.PP
.nf
mitosis -j4 -B
.fi
.PP
Print the plan without executing commands:
.PP
.nf
mitosis --dry-run
.fi
.PP
Record timing data for one build:
.PP
.nf
MITOSIS_GRAPH=1 mitosis
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR tclsh (1)
